<div id="export-panel" style="display: none;">
  <div class="oscar-export">
    <p class="oscar-export-lead">
      This turns your interface into one self-contained page, separate from the
      editor, that keeps working while you carry on editing.
      <strong>Publish</strong> it and OSCAR serves it at an address any phone,
      tablet or computer on the network can open.
      <strong>Download</strong> it to keep the file, host it on a web server,
      or open it on another computer.
    </p>

    <!-- The misunderstanding behind every "my exported page does nothing"
         report, stated before the button rather than after it. -->
    <div class="oscar-export-warning">
      <strong>OSCAR has to be running somewhere the page can reach.</strong>
      A browser cannot send OSC or DMX on its own, so the exported page hands
      everything to OSCAR, which puts it on the network. Keep OSCAR running at
      the address below, on the same network as the device showing the page.
    </div>

    <div id="export-pages" class="oscar-export-warning" style="display: none;">
      <strong>Only the first page is exported.</strong>
      This project has <span id="export-page-count"></span> pages, and an
      exported file is a single surface.
    </div>

    <div class="oscar-export-field">
      <label for="export-name">File name</label>
      <input id="export-name" class="o-input" type="text" placeholder="my-interface" />
    </div>

    <div class="oscar-export-row">
      <div class="oscar-export-field">
        <label for="export-host">OSCAR is reachable at</label>
        <input id="export-host" class="o-input" type="text" spellcheck="false" />
      </div>
      <div class="oscar-export-field">
        <label for="export-port">Bridge port</label>
        <input id="export-port" class="o-input" type="number" min="1" max="65535" />
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="oscar-export-hint">
      Both are filled in with what this OSCAR reports for itself. Use the
      address other devices see, not <code>localhost</code>, unless the page
      will only ever be opened on this computer. If OSCAR moves later there is
      no need to export again: add
      <code>?oscar-host=ADDRESS&amp;oscar-port=PORT</code> to the page's
      address.
    </p>

    <div id="export-error" class="oscar-export-error" style="display: none;"></div>
    <div id="export-note" class="oscar-export-note" style="display: none;"></div>

    <div class="oscar-export-actions">
      <button id="publish-button" class="o-btn o-btn-primary" type="button">Publish on this OSCAR</button>
      <button id="export-button" class="o-btn" type="button">Download the file</button>
    </div>
    <!-- A downloaded page does not work when opened from a phone's storage:
         the phone sandboxes it and it never reaches OSCAR. Said next to the
         button, where the choice is made. -->
    <p class="oscar-export-hint">
      On a phone or tablet, use Publish. A downloaded file only works opened
      on a computer, or from a web server.
    </p>

    <div id="publish-result" class="oscar-publish-result" style="display: none;">
      <div id="publish-qr" class="oscar-publish-qr" role="img" aria-label="QR code for the address"></div>
      <div class="oscar-publish-where">
        <span id="publish-status" class="oscar-publish-status"></span>
        <a id="publish-link" class="o-link oscar-publish-link" target="_blank" rel="noopener"></a>
        <span class="oscar-export-hint">Scan it with the phone's camera, or type the address. The device has to be on the same network.</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div id="published-box" class="oscar-published" style="display: none;">
      <div class="oscar-published-title">Published on this OSCAR</div>
      <ul id="published-list" class="oscar-published-list"></ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
